OK, I'm trying for a long time to achieve atheist six conduct game.
First I tried orcish barbarian, but later switched to a lawful human
priestess. Today, I got another very promising game that ended in
spectacular splat in the Castle.

My priestess started with spellbooks of identify and light. She did
the Mines first, but these were, overall, a disappointment. No magic
lamp, no BoH, only plain sack. Then she did Sokoban, collected some
wands and rings and the big prize, amulet of reflection. During some
later identification session she discovered, that she has got ring of
teleport control, ring of warning, +3 ring of increase damage and --
0:1 wand of wishing. Wand of wishing is only a trinket in a wishless
game, so she bagged it and continued on. She also found the ultra rare
silver spear and after some time, she dipped for Excalibur only to dry
up seven fountains until the Lady finally did her thing. After many
difficult battles (with AC -3 and maxHP in 70 range every troll was a
battle) she reached Medusa. She still had no means of crossing water,
so she dug down. In the maze she landed in, she quickly fell through
hidden trap door. There was another maze level but this time scary
minotaur was approaching fast. Since our brave priestess was keenly
aware that she was no match for that horrible beast and she dug down
again, this time reaching the fabled Castle, rumored to sit on the
entrance to the Hell itself. And hellish place it was, to be sure. The
priestess blinded herself to make sense of her telepathic imagery and
-- to her utmost horror, there was something unimaginable lurking
around. A legendary creature whose existence was denied by many
adventurers - a Castle arch-lich. Having no magic resistance, she
quickly burnt protective E-word with wand of lightning. And it was
just in time, as the skeletal abomination with glowing eyes teleported
to her. She tried to harm it with her Excalibur, but the thing just
teleported to the stairwell and returned healed. It did not dare to
harm her actively, though. Standing on the protective E-word was not
safe, though. Sooner or later the arch-lich would summon something,
that would not be scared off by Elbereth. She needed to do something.
She could not teleport, for that magic doesn't work in these places.
She could not dig anywhere. Leaving the dubious safety of the E square
would leave her vulnerable to deadly necromancy of the arch-lich, to
which she had no protection at all.

After some thinking, she decided to try to reach the upstairs on a
trail of burned E-tiles. That worked until she was very close to the
upstairs. As she was nearly on it, there were few moments she was
vulnerable. The lich wasted no time to cast its magic and our brave
girl struggled to overcome sudden weakness. When she actually reached
the stairs, the lich cast its strength draining spell again and had
particular luck at it. The priestess fell on the stairs massively
weakened from previous St:15 to mere St:3. She was on the edge of
death, but she managed to burn another E-word. With her unicorn horn
she restored her strength and fought on, finally destroying the arch-
lich. She did not realize, there are other effects from the lich
necromancy, that unicorn horn won't cure, much more insidiuous ones.
Her maximum HP were halved. When she stepped out of the protective
Elbereth engraving and killed a rock troll, she attempted to devour
it, so it could not revive. But the meal turned out to be rotten and
she fell unconscious. Meanwhile, the troll came back to life and
quickly bashed our senseless heroine to her untimely death.

Rest in peace, Mandevil the human lawful priestess.

So, in this way I learned, that battling high level spellcasters
without magic resistance is nearly futile endeavor. The spell the arch-
lich used, drain strength, can do insane amount of damage. It drains
strength, but there's one quirk to it, that is not documented on the
nethackwiki website: should the resulting strength fall below 3, the
remaining draining is done on maxHP --- times six! So not only I was
reduced to St:3, my maxHP was halved. The real trouble was I did not
notice as I was completely oblivious that such a thing is possible.
The actual roll for the critical hit was d19, and it seems I got the
worst outcome.


